By keeping only the necessary intermediate states (the last hidden representation, attention scores, etc.) and re‑using them, the decoder can always retrieve a fresh, correct token with higher fidelity. When the speculated token is confirmed wrong, the forward pass can immediately restart with a new hypothesis, but if the token is correct the entire sequence can be emitted, and the decoder never has to recompute the already‑validated part of the transformation.

